    "summary": "Uninitialized memory exposed when streaming decompression of a legacy frame fails",
    "details": "When decompression fails inside a legacy (zstd v0.4 to v0.7) frame, the\nbundled zstd library reports the output buffer as fully written although\nnothing was written. `DCtx::decompress_stream` committed that position even\non error, and for a `Vec<u8>` destination this extended the vector's length\nover uninitialized memory.\n\nLegacy support is enabled by default. A 5- or 6-byte corrupt legacy frame is\nenough: through `zstd::stream::write::Decoder`, up to 32 KiB of stale heap\nmemory is then passed to the wrapped writer on the next `write()` or\n`flush()`, or when an `auto_flush()`/`on_flush()` decoder is dropped. Whether\nthis reaches an attacker depends on where the application sends the\ndecompressed output. `zstd::stream::read::Decoder`, `zstd::decode_all` and\nthe bulk API are not affected.\n\nBefore zstd-safe 4.0.0, output buffers could only be initialized slices.\nThe zstd crate's own writer had the same flaw from 0.4.22 to 0.7.x, which is\ncovered by a separate advisory.\n\nThe flaw was corrected by only committing the buffer positions of a\nsuccessful call.",
    "severity": [],
    "affected": [
      {
        "package": {
          "ecosystem": "crates.io",
          "name": "zstd-safe",
